@ 37.2.2 - Proto-Siouan to Dakota
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Wolff, Hans (1950), “Comparative Siouan I”. International Journal of American Linguistics 16(2):61 – 66; Wolff, Hans (1950), “Comparative Siouan II” International Journal of American Linguistics 16(3):113 – 121; Wolff, Hans (1950), “Comparative Siouan III”. International Journal of American Linguistics 16(4):168 – 178; and cedh audmanh’s changes above, which assisted me in deciphering the vintage phonetic transcription scheme NB: Does not include developments in unstressed non-nasal vowels. L was apparently either /ɹ/ or /l/; Lʲ (Lʸ in the text) was apparently /j/, or maybe /ʎ/. Also, the changes of /p/ before a consonant are unclear, as described within the text.
	{ ç, x } > { x, {ʃ,ʒ} }
	p > p, b, m, w
	tʲ > ʃ / in %
	tʲ > ʒ
	s > z / in %:[-stress]
	s > z / V_V
	r > d / s_
	tʲr > st, ʃt
	r > d / x_
	mn > mVnV / #_
	km > kVmV
	w > p / _t
	t > ∅ / _k, when medial
	hk > t͡ʃ
	k > ∅ / _xV
	x > ʔ / Vk_